# Two things that bit us here, both worth keeping in mind before editing this file:
#   * git only treats a line that *starts* with `#` as a comment. A trailing comment
#     after a pattern becomes part of the pattern, and the rule then matches nothing.
#   * a pattern with no leading slash matches at *every* depth, so a bare `data/`
#     also swallows `jpegai/data/`. Anchor top-level directories with a leading `/`.

# Datasets and weights: large, regenerable, and not what a repo is for.
# `setup.sh` fetches the datasets; `docs/07-training-runbook.md` trains the weights.
# data/ is 4.8 GB (DIV2K, Kodak, Flickr2K); checkpoints/ is 2.9 GB and individual
# files exceed GitHub's 100 MB limit; results/cache/ is rebuilt on the next runbench.
/data/
/results/cache/
# checkpoints/ needs one more layer of care than the others: the *weights* must stay
# out (2.9 GB, individual files over the 100 MB limit) but each ladder.json is a few
# KB and is the only durable record of what a run measured. A rented GPU box gets
# reset, and when one did, eleven checkpoints' numbers went with it -- the losses that
# mattered were kilobytes, not gigabytes.
#
# Hence `/checkpoints/**` and not `/checkpoints/`: an ignored *directory* is never
# descended into, so no negation inside it can rescue anything. Ignoring the contents
# instead leaves the tree walkable, `!/checkpoints/**/` keeps the directories
# themselves visible so git can reach into them, and only then does the last line
# admit the file we want.
/checkpoints/**
!/checkpoints/**/
!/checkpoints/**/ladder.json
# cloud/seeds/ is stripped copies of checkpoints/ for upload to the GPU box -- same
# 100 MB problem, and cloud/make_seed.py regenerates them in seconds. The glob is
# deliberate: --out takes any directory, and seeds_p6/ (114 MB) was already sitting
# untracked next to it, one `git add -A` away from the 100 MB limit.
/cloud/seeds*/
# The same tarball also gets dropped at the root on the way to a browser upload,
# where `/cloud/seeds/` does not cover it. 45 MB, so `git add -A` would otherwise
# push it past GitHub's warning threshold for a file that rebuilds in seconds.
/seeds.tar.gz

# Third-party. The WG1 reference software is a separate project under its own terms;
# `docs/06-normative-constants.md` cites it file-by-file instead of vendoring it.
/ref/

# The paper's own text and figures are IEEE copyright (DOI 10.1109/TCSVT.2025.3613244)
# and are not ours to redistribute. `docs/` cites and paraphrases; the source stays local.
paper/paper_text.txt
paper/imgs/

# The written report is untracked *on purpose*, not by accident. The version that was
# pushed predates the 9-point full-width ladder, so every result in it is stale, and it
# was never reviewed. It is being rewritten from scratch as Phase 14 -- once as the
# report proper, once at more depth for interview preparation. Until then the drafts stay
# local: 240 KB of prose is raw material for that rewrite, and deleting it would cost
# real work, but publishing wrong numbers costs more.
#
# Note for later: `git rm --cached` takes these out of the *current* tree. The old PDF is
# still reachable from history, so if it ever needs to be truly gone that is a separate
# job (filter-repo, force push, and every clone re-cloned).
/report/
/JPEG-AI-Report.pdf
/JPEG-AI-Report.html

# Environment
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Local-machine editor state, not project configuration
.DS_Store

# Training writes these on failure; they are debugging artefacts, sometimes large
nan.pt
scratch_*.py
